Let
$$
\mathfrak a=(XY^3,X^2(Y-3)).
$$
Certainly $\mathfrak a\subseteq(X)$, so $\sqrt{\mathfrak a}\subseteq(X)$. Conversely, cubing the second generator shows that $\mathfrak a$ contains $X^6(Y-3)^3$, while multiplying the first generator by $X^5$ shows that it contains $X^6Y^3$. The ideals generated by $Y^3$ and $(Y-3)^3$ are <comaximal ideals>, so a polynomial combination of the two polynomials is $1$. Multiplication by $X^6$ gives $X^6\in\mathfrak a$, hence $X\in\sqrt{\mathfrak a}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\sqrt{\mathfrak a}=(X)},
$$
generated by the single polynomial $X$.

